  "notes": "Manufacturer: Microchip Technology (formerly Microsemi/PowerDsine). Part of the PD-96xxGC 90W/port IEEE 802.3bt PoE midspan family (PD-9606GC/PD-9612GC/PD-9624GC = 6/12/24 ports respectively, otherwise identical). \"/AC-US\" denotes the US AC power-cord variant only; EU/UK/AU/JP-cord SKUs are electrically/physically identical. Verified via official Microchip datasheet DS00003231E (Rev E, 5/23) and the live product page https://www.microchip.com/en-us/product/pd-9624gc (both fetched through Internet Archive mirrors since the live microchip.com host returned 403 to automated fetches; content is current as of Oct/Nov 2025 crawls). Data rate, connector types, AC/DC input specs and dimensions are stated explicitly in the datasheet (high confidence). However, the datasheet only states \"Number of Ports: 24\" and generically describes \"Ports: 6-Port Gang-Shielded RJ-45\" without an explicit front-panel port diagram. The 24 unpowered \"Data In\" + 24 powered \"PoE Out\" split (48 total Ethernet jacks) reflects the standard architecture of this Microsemi/PowerDsine midspan family (a midspan requires a separate pass-through input jack per channel since it does not integrate a switch), and the same 438x272x44mm 1U chassis has been reused across this product's generations that are documented with this in/out layout — but it is inferred, not itemized in this datasheet, hence medium overall confidence. Management/console RJ-45 and USB Type-A \"Communication Port\" speed/version are not stated, so grade is omitted for those. DC input is a screw terminal block (2 positive + 2 negative terminals, nominal 55V per the prior-gen 2019 datasheet DS00003231A) mapped to the closest enum value \"euroblock-dc\"; it is used for external battery backup / current-sharing power and applies to the 9612GC/9624GC models (not the 6-port 9606GC). AC inlet is IEC 60320 C14 (datasheet has an apparent typo \"IEC 69320\"), mapped to \"iec\" per the C13/14 convention.",
